00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.680
I'm going to be speaking with&nbsp;
Jonathan Berman, who's a professor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:10.240
national co-chair of the Twenty Seventeen March&nbsp;
for Science and also author of Anti Vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.560
How to Challenge a Misinformed Movement.
Jonathan, so great to have you on today.&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.920
Thank you. Thank you for having me.
So let's start to start with my conception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.120
of the anti vaccine movement, which which&nbsp;
I've studied over time, is that you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:33.200
right and left wing so-called anti vaccine folks,&nbsp;
often motivated in different ways. I tend to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:39.280
the the right wing anti vaccine is&nbsp;
more based in skepticism of government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.640
whereas the left wing anti vaccine is more&nbsp;
skepticism of big pharma. So first question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.640
am I right that this sometimes crosses political&nbsp;
lines? Is it along the lines I laid out?&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.360
Yeah, for the most part, so up until very&nbsp;
recently, there wasn't a big difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:05.280
left and right as far as vaccine skepticism&nbsp;
over the last few years, there's been a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:12.080
big rightward shift. I just was reading&nbsp;
a paper indicating that there's a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:20.680
the second strongest predictor of anti vaccine&nbsp;
sentiment right now is Christian nationalism. Hmm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:28.080
So there have been some some change changes in the&nbsp;
demographic makeup of the entire vaccine movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.400
like you would expect with a big&nbsp;
event like the pandemic going on.&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.080
What would you say? So currently, maybe&nbsp;
we'll start now and maybe work backwards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.360
if it makes sense. We've seen vaccine&nbsp;
skepticism over the coronavirus vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.680
decline overall over the last year. I think there&nbsp;
was a point last year where something like 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.000
percent of Americans said they weren't interested&nbsp;
in that dropped to 50 in the latest numbers I saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:59.600
about twenty eight. Twenty eight or 30 percent. Is&nbsp;
the is the current situation unique historically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.480
or is it typical that when there is the&nbsp;
resurgence of a new communicable disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:12.240
that there is more hesitancy that declines?
You know, I was initially skeptical of those those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:20.880
survey numbers, but isn't that for&nbsp;
diseases like measles, we we've seen a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.000
high degree of skepticism on surveys. But then&nbsp;
when it comes when when the rubber hits the road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.240
when it's time to actually get vaccinated, we see&nbsp;
very high numbers of vaccination, high compliance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:40.320
So there's there's something about the way those&nbsp;
surveys tend to be written or something about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:51.680
way that the people respond to them, that. That&nbsp;
makes people a little bit hesitant to say they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:58.720
going to get it, but then get it. So when it was&nbsp;
saying 40 percent, 30, I think it's going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.240
higher than that. So that's not surprising&nbsp;
that as people get vaccinated, people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:12.320
r- relaxing a little bit about their skepticism.
One of the things I would imagine will will move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.960
some people who might genuinely be on the&nbsp;
skeptical side to the I want the vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:21.600
side would be if airlines, for example,&nbsp;
say after a certain date you'll need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.040
vaccinated to fly with us, or you can avoid&nbsp;
a quarantine if you're vaccinated or if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:31.120
want to come to an event where tickets are&nbsp;
sold by Ticketmaster Live Nation, you'll need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.800
to be vaccinated. I think that that that will&nbsp;
certainly be a factor. But beyond those sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:43.120
I want to be able to do X type of things, what&nbsp;
tends to bring people out of vaccine skepticism?&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:50.720
Right. So as far as what governments can do, like&nbsp;
you said, you have these noncoercive measures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:58.000
and ideally we don't want to be coercing people&nbsp;
because that's when people become most resistant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:05.040
is when they feel like they're being coerced.&nbsp;
So. What what seems to help is seeing people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:14.320
belong to the same groups as them getting&nbsp;
vaccinated, people who have similar backgrounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:22.720
similar socioeconomic status, similar race and&nbsp;
religion, getting vaccinated in the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.720
So, for example, there is strong vaccine&nbsp;
hesitancy in the black community, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:38.640
but that's partially predicated on the history of&nbsp;
medical racism and addressing that is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.240
an important part of persuading people in that&nbsp;
community, maybe less important for persuading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:54.400
Orthodox Jews or or white evangelicals. So&nbsp;
I think the most important thing we can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.640
is to sort of target our messaging&nbsp;
to the groups we're speaking to.&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.560
That's interesting, yeah, the three&nbsp;
currently most vaccine hesitant groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.320
that I looked at, and again, this is as you&nbsp;
mentioned, if we can if we can trust the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:14.800
it's black Americans, Republicans&nbsp;
and those without college degrees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:24.080
what would it be that would be more likely to&nbsp;
be convincing to, for example, just Republicans?&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.360
Right.
So there is this moral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:34.960
foundations theory in psychology that says that&nbsp;
people who tend to be more conservative respond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:41.680
more to certain moral foundations, that people&nbsp;
tend to be more prone to other moral foundations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:52.480
So the ones that conservatives, I think, respond&nbsp;
more to our sort of disgust, loyalty. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:59.360
we want to frame messaging around those moral&nbsp;
foundations, in my opinion. So we would want to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.160
you know, talk about when people say these&nbsp;
are chemicals, these are outside substances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.600
you're putting in your body, what's&nbsp;
really in there? What does it mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:19.840
what's it there for? And then have the discussion&nbsp;
with them about bodily autonomy, control over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.720
what medical treatments are being performed on&nbsp;
them, and make sure it's clear that this is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.960
a case of government overreach&nbsp;
and that public health measures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:38.080
are an important part of the functions of&nbsp;
government that they should hopefully accept.&nbsp;

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:43.040
Yeah, Jonathan, Jonathan Haidt has written&nbsp;
a lot about what you know, as you point out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:48.320
the values that are more relevant and&nbsp;
motivational to different different groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:55.040
of people when it comes to vaccine skepticism.&nbsp;
Are there examples from history where it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:01.520
justified to be skeptical of a vaccine?
So there have been instances in history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:09.440
where things have gone wrong and there were&nbsp;
mistakes made in manufacturing of a vaccine or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.360
we're welcoming we're welcoming your&nbsp;
dog to the interview now, which is fine.&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:22.880
The audience loves dogs.
CDC is a see someone outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:29.200
delivering a package and she is very angry.&nbsp;
I said send her to safety. There she is.&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.000
So, yeah, there have been instances in the past&nbsp;
where mistakes have been made or in manufacturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:43.840
or where in the 70s there was a flu pandemic&nbsp;
that was predicted that never came about.&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:52.000
And there was a vaccine that very slightly&nbsp;
increased the risk of ironbar syndrome. Right. So.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:08:01.040
Those things have happened in the past, and that's&nbsp;
why it's not unreasonable to be cautious, right?&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:07.760
So that's why we we test vaccines with thousands&nbsp;
and thousands of people before they come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:13.280
market. And that's why these things are reviewed&nbsp;
by government panels. And these are you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:20.240
the saying is regulations are written in blood. A&nbsp;
lot of these regulations have come about because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:29.760
of those mistakes. Really big, important one&nbsp;
was the cut in Wyeth Labs incident in the 1950s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:40.640
There was about 200000 doses of polio vaccine.&nbsp;
Right. And the that vaccine design involves live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:45.520
polio virus that was inactivated. It was it wasn't&nbsp;
killed because viruses aren't alive, but it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:53.840
made unable to infect people with I believe it&nbsp;
was formaldehyde and it was improperly done. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:02.880
this this vaccine that was sent out was able&nbsp;
to infect people. And so about 200 people had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:10.240
a paralyzed limb where they were injected or&nbsp;
with with that vaccine. And so that led to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:15.760
creation of some of these government agencies&nbsp;
that are responsible for that regulation.&nbsp;

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:22.080
You know, one that I've been getting maybe, you&nbsp;
know, 10 or 12 emails about over the last few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:27.760
weeks, as we've talked about vaccines, is folks&nbsp;
who write to me and say, you know, David, clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:35.840
vaccines can do harm because there is this vaccine&nbsp;
injury compensation apparatus that the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:42.720
has has created and that that wouldn't be&nbsp;
there if this wasn't a thing. So therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:49.680
therefore, what I guess is the question.
Right. So the the National Vaccine Injury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:55.600
Compensation Program came about in the&nbsp;
1980s and there had been a paper in 1974&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:06.800
that alleged or seemed that seemed to show that&nbsp;
there was from the vaccine an increased risk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:16.320
neurological problems. And so that led to people&nbsp;
saying, oh, is that true? And a bunch of studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:22.320
eventually figured out, no, it's not true. But&nbsp;
the consequences of that were a wave of countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:31.840
deciding that they would no longer no&nbsp;
longer vaccinate for I'm sorry, DPT vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:40.160
deteriorates process, they would no longer&nbsp;
vaccinate for pertussis. And there was a wave of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:46.400
outbreaks that followed in those countries where&nbsp;
pertussis came back, whooping cough, a lot of sick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:53.120
kids. And in the U.S., there are only a handful&nbsp;
of companies that manufactured pertussis vaccine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:59.360
So lawyers saw this and they decided, OK,&nbsp;
we're going to start suing these companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:05.440
manufacturing, Deepti, and we'll use this paper&nbsp;
as evidence. And so there were there were at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.600
least one, I believe, possibly two companies&nbsp;
that went out and said, we're not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:14.800
make it anymore. It's too big a liability,&nbsp;
will be sued out of existence. And Congress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:21.120
saw this and they realized that if we continue&nbsp;
allowing people to sue based on flimsy evidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:29.760
these vaccine manufacturers, we won't have a&nbsp;
vaccine supply. So they kind of came up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:37.920
this idea that's not perfect and which is the&nbsp;
National Vaccine Injury Compensation Program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:47.040
so excuse me, if you are vaccinated, then in&nbsp;
the next few days you develop certain symptoms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:53.440
You are certain that they have&nbsp;
defined as possible vaccine injury.&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:12:00.320
They will compensate you and you can go&nbsp;
to the vaccine court and the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:05.440
will compensate you instead of suing the&nbsp;
vaccine manufacturer out of existence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:10.480
And that's not perfect because that&nbsp;
takes away some of the liability if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:14.640
they actually do do something wrong.
Right. And how common are the claims?&nbsp;

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:20.880
I don't have the number off the top of my head,&nbsp;
it's fairly common for people to file claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:28.720
they have paid out a lot over time. The&nbsp;
trouble is that the standard of evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:34.160
required by the vaccine court is very different&nbsp;
from the scientific standard of evidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:42.160
I or other scientists would require to to say&nbsp;
this is this is demonstrated to my satisfaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:49.360
as provisionally true in the lab.
Right, so it's a standard that that allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:56.560
maybe for more successful filings&nbsp;
than a a more rigorous method would.&nbsp;

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:03.760
Yeah, so legal proceedings don't always&nbsp;
track with scientific methods. Right.&nbsp;

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:10.320
Yeah, listen, last thing I wanted to touch on&nbsp;
when it comes to the rollout of the coronavirus&nbsp;&nbsp;

